{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 2,
  "title": "Prop 2 \u2014 Rainy-day reserves: save more, or spend now?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 5252,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://225ac463733d98bf09858a9ab1ea50ce39324163db9879146610b225faa5f87e.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition two changes how California saves for bad budget years. State revenue depends heavily on income taxes, including investment gains, so a falling stock market can leave a large hole in the budget. The rainy-day fund is a reserve account, not a new tax. Current rules stop requiring additional deposits when that account reaches ten percent of General Fund tax revenues. Proposition two raises that threshold to twenty percent and requires more saving in particularly strong investment-tax years. It changes the rules over time; it does not instantly double the money already saved."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a school manager planning through an economic downturn. Think of a household that saves when overtime pay is high. If those hours disappear, savings can cover bills. A state reserve serves a similar purpose, although public budgets and legal rules are more complicated. Public services need budgets that can weather weak years."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "For illustration, ten percent of one hundred dollars is ten dollars; twenty percent is twenty. The higher threshold creates room for a larger required cushion, not a requirement to put away twenty percent every year. The measure also extends required extra debt payments from twenty thirty through twenty forty, and expands the debts those payments can address. These are extra payments beyond ordinary repayment schedules. Deposits would generally count against the state spending limit when withdrawn rather than when saved. Emergency rules still allow deposits to stop or reserves to be used. The analyst expects higher reserves, without a fixed dollar forecast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might prefer a stronger buffer against service cuts, emergency borrowing, or tax increases during a downturn. Paying debt earlier can also reduce future interest. The appeal is more resilience when revenue is unpredictable."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask what saving more leaves unfunded today. Another voter could prioritize services or infrastructure now. Saving more means less available for current uses, and changing spending-limit treatment can affect when excess revenue must be returned or allocated. A larger reserve also cannot eliminate every recession shortfall."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 60 percent said yes, 39 percent no, and 1 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes changes reserve and extra-debt-payment rules; no keeps the current rules. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
